<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suryavarman II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1113">1113</a>–c. 1150</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suryavarman II was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> in the first half of the twelfth century. He built the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> and campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> and other neighbours. Accounts of the Khmer Empire treat his reign as a high point of Khmer power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suryavarman-ii.jpg" alt="Suryavarman II" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Gunther · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suryavarman_II_Angkor_Wat_0869.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Suryavarman II, whose name begins with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> root meaning sun, grew up on a provincial estate at a time of weakening central control in the empire. An inscription names his father as Ksitindraditya and his mother as Narendralakshmi. As a young prince he contended that he had a legitimate claim to the throne. He appears to have dealt with a rival claimant from the line of Harshavarman III, probably Nripatindravarman, and then to have turned on the elderly king Dharanindravarman I, his great uncle; scholars disagree about which of the two an inscription describes him killing.</p><p>In 1114 he sent a mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulothunga Chola I</a> in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He was enthroned in 1113, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> sage Divakarapandita officiating, and formally crowned in 1119.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>During his decades in power the king reunited the empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> paid him tribute. His large military operations against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a> and Dai Viet were largely unsuccessful. Vietnamese and Cham sources record three major attacks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghe An</a> and Quang Binh provinces. In 1128 he is said to have led 20,000 soldiers against Dai Viet and been defeated, and the next year sent a fleet of more than 700 vessels against its coast.</p><p>In 1145 he appears to have invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, sacked its capital Vijaya and placed a new king, Harideva, on the throne, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> forces under Jaya Harivarman I recaptured the capital and killed Harideva. In 1116 he resumed formal relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in 1128 the emperor conferred high dignities on him as a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the empire.</p><p>He presided over the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, whose five central towers evoke the peaks of Mount Meru. Other temples of his reign include Banteay Samre, Thommanon, Chau Say Tevoda and Beng Mealea. He made Vishnu, rather than Shiva, the focus of court religion, which was unusual among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> kings; scholars debate whether this explains why Angkor Wat faces west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Inscriptions suggest that Suryavarman II died around 1150, possibly during a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, after his troops had been defeated by Vietnamese forces under Tô Hiến Thành. His cousin Dharanindravarman II succeeded him, and a period of weak rule and feuding began. He was given the posthumous name Paramavishnuloka, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> appears to have been completed only after his death. He is the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king depicted in art, seated on a dais in a bas-relief in the south gallery of Angkor Wat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suryavarman II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Expansion reached its height under rulers such as <b>Suryavarman II</b>, who campaigned against Dai Viet and Champa, and Jayavarman VII.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Angkor Wat, built in the early 12th century for the Khmer king <b>Suryavarman II</b>, was dedicated to Vishnu, and the temples of Prambanan in Java to Shiva in the 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Angkor Wat was commissioned by the Khmer king <b>Suryavarman II</b> (ruled 1113–c. 1150) in the early 12th century at Yaśodharapura, present-day Angkor, the capital of the Khmer Empire.</blockquote><a href="/work/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=1131" aria-label="Angkor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7396 1663 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Suryavarman II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7846 1933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7846 1933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.43,103.83&amp;year=1131">What was here in 1131? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
